(* tuvm#94 — the ship's-bell clock. Cumulative appliance node.

   STAGE 2 (committed 2610105): the bell strikes on the half-hour from a local
   timer. No network; the claim is that the bell ALWAYS rings.
   STAGE 3 (fa4fc78): the e-paper shows VM stats, repainted after each striking.
   Together stages 1-3 are a FULLY WORKING APPLIANCE that merely drifts — WiFi
   (stage 4) becomes an accuracy upgrade, not a prerequisite. Stage 3 runs at the
   full 64 KB arena, so the RAM risk stays confined to stage 4.
   STAGE 4 (here): NTP catches the counter up to real Phoenix time — COUNT and
   PHASE — on every sync. The network is allowed to improve the time and is never
   allowed to stop the bell: a failed sync is a nil the node shrugs off and keeps
   free-running on its own crystal.

   THE CADENCE IS BACK TO A REAL HALF-HOUR, and this is stage 4's entry price.
   Stages 2-3 ran a 300000 ms tick — a 6x demo clock, where one tick still advanced
   a half-hour of CLOCK time so a full watch took 40 bench-minutes instead of 4
   hours. That is incoherent the moment NTP arrives: syncing the count to real time
   and then advancing it six times too fast means the panel is wrong within minutes
   and every sync yanks it back. Real-time alignment REQUIRES one tick to BE one
   real half-hour, so `tickMillis` is 1800000 here. The 6x affordance dies with the
   drift it was compensating for; BUTTON_B still fast-forwards for the bench.

   THE CLOCK IS A COUNTER, NOT A TIMESTAMP. `halfHours` counts half-hours from
   midnight, 0..47, and `bootTicks` counts them from boot and NEVER wraps.
   Deriving either from `monotonicMillis` was the obvious alternative and is worse
   twice: it is an SMI on this part, so it wraps after ~12.4 days on a device meant
   to run for months, and every strike would then ride arithmetic that has to stay
   right across that wrap. Uptime therefore comes from `bootTicks` (30-minute
   granularity, honest — the panel only repaints that often) rather than millis.

   THE COLLISION THAT MUST NOT BE REINTRODUCED (design D-collision). The repaint
   must NOT share a tick with the strike. A full e-paper refresh BLOCKS for
   seconds, and the bell is a HARDWARE oscillator — so a repaint landing mid-strike
   does not merely delay the bell, it leaves the tone DRONING, because the decay
   steps that silence it are stuck behind the repaint. `advanceAndStrike:` answers
   this by ORDERING, not machinery: strike to completion, THEN repaint. No async.

   PAINT IS BEST-EFFORT, THE BELL IS NOT. Every paint is wrapped so a board with no
   panel (or a panel fault) carries on ringing headless — the same "carry on
   headless" value the display seam was built for (#88). `hasDisplay` is probed
   once at boot so the per-paint path is a plain flag test.

   NO BOOT STRIKE (stage 2 decision), but there IS a boot PAINT: the panel would
   otherwise be blank for the first 30 minutes, before the appliance has shown it
   works. A paint is not a strike, so this does not weaken the "the timer rings the
   bell" claim. `bootTicks = 0` is the "no strike yet" signal the bells row reads,
   so the panel does not claim a striking that has not happened.

   BUTTON_A repeats the last striking (stage 1's gate, kept as regression + the
   liveness check that covers the silent first interval; `lastBells` seeds to 8).
   BUTTON_B fast-forwards one half-hour through THE SAME method the timer calls —
   a witness for the timer, not a second implementation. Bench affordance; the one
   thing here that would not ship. The real 30-min cadence proves itself in the
   console log over hours.

   `every:` not a rescheduled `after:`: the Zephyr backend arms a PERIODIC k_timer
   (timer_zephyr.c:66), re-armed from the previous EXPIRY, so a ~2.5-4 s striking
   eats into the interval but cannot shift the schedule — no drift to correct.

   Grid is portrait 12x18 (CFB font1016 on a 128x296 panel). 12 columns is a HARD
   limit; a longer line raises #displayBounds (#89), so every row is built to
   exactly 12 by `line:val:`. This node never returns, so it drains forever and
   cannot leak a live repeater the way TimerNode could. *)
class MagTagClockNode packageUsing: manifest = (
  | plat timers halfHours ::= 0. bootTicks ::= 0. lastBells ::= 8. hasDisplay ::= false.
    tick phaseCh ntpCh strikeTok phaseTok ntpTok.
    synced ::= false. lastCorr ::= 0. corrMin ::= 0. corrMax ::= 0. haveCorr ::= false.
    syncCount ::= 0. | ) (

  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space btnA btnB |
    plat:: platform.
    space:: plat space.

    (* Probe the panel ONCE — carry on headless if there is none. *)
    [ plat display clear. hasDisplay:: true ]
      on: [ :e | e tag = #displayUnavailable ]
      do: [ :e | 'no display; running headless' out ].

    (* HOLD ONE Timers INSTANCE — this is load-bearing, and getting it wrong is
       why the 30-minute timer never fired for an entire night (2026-07-23/24).
       `plat timers` answers a FRESH Timers on EVERY call, and each instance owns
       its own `pending` registry (token -> chan/msg). `dispatch:` does
       `pending at: tokenEvent ifAbsent: [nil]` and then SILENTLY DOES NOTHING when
       absent. So registering on one instance and dispatching on another means the
       k_timer fires, the ISR posts, the reaction runs — and drops the event on the
       floor, with no crash and no output. The failure is INVISIBLE.
       An earlier revision of this comment said re-fetching for dispatch was "fine";
       it stated the correct fact (only the registering instance holds the token)
       and then drew the opposite conclusion. TimerNode.nsl warns about exactly this.
       Both the registration below and the dispatch above must be THIS instance. *)
    timers:: plat timers.
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].
    tick:: space channel: 'clock/tick'.
    tick whenever: '' do: [ :m | self advanceAndStrike ].
    strikeTok:: timers every: self tickMillis post: #tick on: tick.

    (* THE PHASE ONE-SHOT GETS ITS OWN CHANNEL, and that is a deliberate departure
       from the plan's sketch, which re-armed the periodic inside advanceAndStrike
       whenever `strikeTok == nil`. That reads the token as a mode flag, and
       advanceAndStrike has a SECOND caller — BUTTON_B. A press landing in the window
       between a re-phase and its boundary would have re-armed the periodic while the
       one-shot was still pending, leaving two strike timers running forever. Giving
       the boundary its own channel keeps advanceAndStrike a pure "one half-hour
       happened" and puts the re-arm where it belongs. *)
    phaseCh:: space channel: 'clock/phase'.
    phaseCh whenever: '' do: [ :m | self onPhaseBoundary ].

    (* NTP: retry every 30 s until the first success, then re-arm at the half-hour.
       Registered BEFORE the boot sync below so applySync: has a token to cancel. *)
    ntpCh:: space channel: 'clock/ntp'.
    ntpCh whenever: '' do: [ :m | self trySync ].
    ntpTok:: timers every: self bootRetryMillis post: #ntp on: ntpCh.

    btnA:: space channel: 'btn/a'.
    btnA whenever: '' do: [ :m |
      ('repeat ' , lastBells printString) out.
      (plat bell) strike: lastBells ].
    plat gpio pin: 15 edge: #falling post: 'A' on: btnA.

    btnB:: space channel: 'btn/b'.
    btnB whenever: '' do: [ :m | self advanceAndStrike ].
    plat gpio pin: 14 edge: #falling post: 'B' on: btnB.

    (* Boot paint: show the appliance is alive before the first tick. *)
    self paintStats.
    ('clock ready, ' , self tickMillis printString , ' ms per tick (1 tick = one half-hour of clock time)') out.

    (* One sync attempt BEFORE the drain loop, rather than waiting out the first
       30 s retry. It blocks up to the SNTP timeout, which is free here — nothing is
       scheduled yet and no bell can be late. A board that comes up already on the
       network is therefore aligned before its first strike, which is the behaviour
       an appliance should have after a power cut. *)
    self trySync.

    [ true ] whileTrue: [ plat pumpEvents. space drain ].
  )

  (* --- NTP: count and phase ---------------------------------------------- *)

  (* Attempt one sync. nil -> leave the clock free-running and try again in the next
     window; there is no error path here BY DESIGN (see the kernel forwarder). *)
  trySync = (
    | secs |
    secs:: plat ntpSyncTz: self tzOffset.
    secs == nil ifTrue: [ 'ntp miss' out. ^ self ].
    self applySync: secs.
    ^ self )

  (* THE CATCHUP, both halves. COUNT: `halfHours` jumps to the true half-hour index.
     PHASE: the strike timer is re-aimed at the true boundary, because a count that
     is right while the strikes land at boot-relative offsets is still a clock that
     rings at the wrong minute. *)
  applySync: secs = (
    | newHalf into msToBoundary |
    newHalf:: secs // 1800.
    into:: secs - (newHalf * 1800).              (* seconds into the current half-hour *)
    (* A FULL interval at a boundary, never 0. At secs exactly on a boundary the
       naive reading is "0 ms to the next one", which fires a one-shot immediately
       and strikes the same half-hour twice. Landing on a boundary is not a rare
       edge here — it is what a good sync produces. *)
    msToBoundary:: 1800000 - (into * 1000).

    (* Record the drift only once already synced: the FIRST sync's jump is an initial
       reset, not drift, and logging it would peg the 24 h window to a meaningless
       spike. *)
    synced ifTrue: [ self recordCorrection: (self correctionTo: newHalf) ].

    halfHours:: newHalf.
    ('ntp sync -> ' , (self timeString: halfHours) , ', boundary in ' , msToBoundary printString , ' ms') out.
    self rephaseStrike: msToBoundary.

    synced ifFalse: [
      synced:: true.
      (* The 30 s boot retry has done its job; sync at the half-hour from here. *)
      ntpTok == nil ifFalse: [ timers cancel: ntpTok ].
      ntpTok:: timers every: 1800000 post: #ntp on: ntpCh ].
    ^ self )

  (* How far the local count had drifted, in seconds, signed (+ = local ran ahead).
     WRAPPED, which the plan's `(halfHours - newHalf) * 1800` was not: across
     midnight a local 47 against a true 0 is a one-half-hour correction, not a
     47-half-hour one, and the unwrapped form would poison the 24 h window with an
     84600 s spike exactly once a day. *)
  correctionTo: newHalf = (
    | d |
    d:: halfHours - newHalf.
    d > 24 ifTrue: [ d:: d - 48 ].
    d < -24 ifTrue: [ d:: d + 48 ].
    ^ d * 1800 )

  (* Re-aim the strike at the true boundary: drop the periodic, one-shot to the
     boundary, and let onPhaseBoundary resume the periodic from there. Any one-shot
     still pending from an earlier sync is cancelled first — two syncs inside one
     interval would otherwise leave two boundary timers armed.
     `cancel:` removes the token from the Timers registry, so an event ALREADY
     queued for a cancelled token is dropped by `dispatch:`. That silent drop is the
     very bug that cost a night in stage 2; here it is exactly the protection
     wanted, and it is why re-phasing cannot double-strike. *)
  rephaseStrike: msToBoundary = (
    strikeTok == nil ifFalse: [ timers cancel: strikeTok. strikeTok:: nil ].
    phaseTok == nil ifFalse: [ timers cancel: phaseTok. phaseTok:: nil ].
    phaseTok:: timers after: msToBoundary post: #tick on: phaseCh.
    ^ self )

  (* The true boundary arrived: strike it, then resume the periodic from here — from
     now on the half-hours are real ones. *)
  onPhaseBoundary = (
    phaseTok:: nil.
    self advanceAndStrike.
    strikeTok == nil ifTrue: [
      strikeTok:: timers every: self tickMillis post: #tick on: tick ].
    ^ self )

  (* Rolling 24 h window = 48 syncs at one an hour... at one every half-hour, 48 is
     24 h exactly. Reset by counting rather than by keeping a ring of samples: the
     panel reports an extreme, not a series, so the samples would be RAM spent to
     produce a number nobody reads — and this is the rung that can least afford it. *)
  recordCorrection: corr = (
    lastCorr:: corr.
    syncCount:: syncCount + 1.
    syncCount >= 48 ifTrue: [ syncCount:: 0. haveCorr:: false ].
    haveCorr ifFalse: [ corrMin:: corr. corrMax:: corr. haveCorr:: true. ^ self ].
    corr < corrMin ifTrue: [ corrMin:: corr ].
    corr > corrMax ifTrue: [ corrMax:: corr ].
    ^ self )

  (* seconds -> signed '+N.0' / '-N.0'. HONEST COARSE READOUT (design): corr is the
     correction the COUNT needed, quantised to half-hours, so in practice this reads
     '+0.0' and means the crystal held alignment. The '.0' is cosmetic — the counter
     has no sub-second notion of time and this rung deliberately does not spend the
     RAM to get one. A NON-ZERO value is the alarm. *)
  corrField: sec = (
    | s a |
    s:: sec < 0 ifTrue: [ '-' ] ifFalse: [ '+' ].
    a:: sec < 0 ifTrue: [ 0 - sec ] ifFalse: [ sec ].
    ^ s , a printString , '.0' )

  ntpField = (
    haveCorr ifTrue: [ ^ (self corrField: lastCorr) , 's' ].
    synced ifTrue: [ ^ 'sync' ].
    ^ '--' )

  (* THE ONE PATH THE TIMER AND BUTTON_B SHARE. Strike THEN paint — never the
     reverse and never concurrently (see the collision note in the header). *)
  advanceAndStrike = (
    halfHours:: halfHours + 1.
    halfHours = 48 ifTrue: [ halfHours:: 0 ].   (* wrap at midnight *)
    bootTicks:: bootTicks + 1.                  (* uptime counter, never wraps *)
    lastBells:: (plat bell) bellsForHalfHour: halfHours.
    ((self timeString: halfHours) , ' strike ' , lastBells printString) out.
    (plat bell) strike: lastBells.
    'struck' out.
    self paintStats.
    ^ self )

  (* Build the panel and push it. Best-effort: a panel that faults mid-paint must
     not stop the clock, so the whole thing is caught. Each row is ALSO echoed to
     the console, so the capture log witnesses exactly what was laid onto the grid
     without needing a photograph — the device seam does not print rows the way
     the host seam does. *)
  (* NO Array HERE. An app node's `platform` is the Kernel instance, so `Array` /
     `basicNew:indexable:` are NOT reachable from application code (they resolve at
     compile time and then fail at run time) — the rows are therefore put directly,
     one row per call, rather than staged in a collection. *)
  paintStats = (
    hasDisplay ifFalse: [ ^ self ].
    [ | disp hs |
      disp:: plat display.
      hs:: plat heapStats.
      disp clear.
      (* '+' suffix: the counter resolves to the half-hour, so the shown time is a
         FLOOR — the true time is this boundary OR LATER (up to :29 past), never
         before. 'HH:MM+' says so honestly. timeString: is already 24-hour. *)
      self put: (self line: 'oevm'  val: ((self timeString: halfHours) , '+')) row: 0 on: disp.
      self put: '----------'                                           row: 1 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: 'heap'  val: (self heapField: hs))         row: 2 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: 'gc'    val: hs gcCycles printString)      row: 3 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: 'up'    val: self upField)                 row: 4 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: 'bells' val: self bellsField)              row: 5 on: disp.
      self put: '----------'                                           row: 6 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: 'ntp'   val: self ntpField)                row: 7 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: '24h'   val: (haveCorr ifTrue: [ self corrField: corrMax ] ifFalse: [ '--' ])) row: 8 on: disp.
      self put: (self line: ''      val: (haveCorr ifTrue: [ self corrField: corrMin ] ifFalse: [ '' ]))   row: 9 on: disp.
      disp refresh ]
    on: [ :e | (e tag = #displayUnavailable) or: [ (e tag = #displayFault) or: [ e tag = #displayBounds ] ] ]
    do: [ :e | ('paint skipped: ' , e tag printString) out ].
    ^ self )

  (* Put one row and echo it — the echo is the console witness of what was laid
     onto the grid, since the device display seam (unlike the host's) does not
     print rows. *)
  put: s row: r on: disp = (
    disp at: r col: 0 put: s.
    ('| ' , s) out.
    ^ self )

  (* --- field formatters ------------------------------------------------- *)

  (* live/size in KiB, live ROUNDED to nearest whole K. The decimal cost the one
     column that separated the label from the value ('heap18/64K' was flush); at
     whole-K the value is at most 'NN/64K' (6 chars), so `line:val:` always has >=2
     columns of padding and the row reads 'heap  18/64K'. At the 64 KB arena size
     reads 64K; stage 4's smaller arena needs no change here. *)
  heapField: hs = (
    ^ (self kRound: hs liveBytes) , '/' , (hs heapSize // 1024) printString , 'K' )

  (* Uptime from bootTicks, wrap-safe. 30-min granularity is honest: the panel
     only repaints that often, so a finer figure would be invented precision. *)
  upField = (
    | mins d r h m |
    (* REAL elapsed minutes, derived from the cadence — NOT a hardcoded 30. Written
       when the tick was 300000 (where a hardcoded 30 would have claimed six hours of
       uptime per bench hour); stage 4 moved the cadence back to 1800000 and this
       line needed no edit, which is the point of deriving it.
       Approximate by up to one interval after an NTP re-phase, since the boundary
       one-shot is a partial interval counted as a whole tick. At 30-minute
       granularity that is inside the rounding the row already admits to. *)
    mins:: bootTicks * (self tickMillis // 60000).
    d:: mins // 1440.
    r:: mins - (d * 1440).
    h:: r // 60.
    m:: r - (h * 60).
    ^ d printString , 'd' , (self pad: h) , ':' , (self pad: m) )

  (* bootTicks = 0 means no strike has happened yet, so the panel says so rather
     than parroting the seeded lastBells and claiming a striking that never rang. *)
  bellsField = ( bootTicks = 0 ifTrue: [ ^ '--' ]. ^ lastBells printString )

  (* --- primitives -------------------------------------------------------- *)

  (* Right-justify `v` against `label` in exactly 12 columns. Overshoot would
     raise #displayBounds at paint time — a loud signal that a field grew too wide,
     which paintStats catches rather than letting it stop the clock. *)
  line: label val: v = (
    | pad |
    pad:: 12 - label size - v size.
    pad < 0 ifTrue: [ pad:: 0 ].
    ^ label , (self spaces: pad) , v )

  spaces: n = ( | s | s:: ''. n timesRepeat: [ s:: s , ' ' ]. ^ s )

  (* Round bytes to nearest whole KiB: +512 before the floor-divide. nsl has no
     rounding operator, so this is the standard bias-then-floor. *)
  kRound: bytes = ( ^ ((bytes + 512) // 1024) printString )

  timeString: i = (
    | h |
    h:: i // 2.
    ^ (self pad: h) , (((i - (h * 2)) = 0) ifTrue: [ ':00' ] ifFalse: [ ':30' ]) )

  pad: n = ( (n < 10) ifTrue: [ ^ '0' , n printString ]. ^ n printString )

  (* THE TICK CADENCE — back to a real half-hour for stage 4.
     Stages 2-3 ran 300000 (a 6x demo clock) to make a full 8-bell watch cost 40
     bench-minutes instead of 4 hours, and the stage-3 comment recorded that stage 4
     would have to reconcile it. This is that reconciliation, and it is the honest
     branch of the two the comment offered: NTP aligns the count to REAL time, so a
     tick must BE a real half-hour. The alternative — keeping 300000 and carrying an
     explicit tick->clock-time mapping — would mean a clock that is deliberately
     wrong between syncs and yanked back by each one, which is not a clock.
     What is lost is only bench speed, and only for this build. BUTTON_B still
     fast-forwards a half-hour through the same path the timer uses, so the striking
     and the panel are still exercisable in seconds; what now takes real time is the
     one thing that always did — proving the TIMER fires unattended (stages 2-3,
     witnessed 2026-07-24). *)
  tickMillis = ( ^ 1800000 )   (* 30 minutes — a real half-hour *)

  (* Phoenix, UTC-7, no DST — correct year-round, which is why a fixed offset is
     honest here and would not be in a DST zone. A product fact, so it lives in nsl
     and is passed into the prim rather than compiled into C. *)
  tzOffset = ( ^ -25200 )

  (* Boot NTP retry cadence, until the first success flips `synced`. *)
  bootRetryMillis = ( ^ 30000 )
) : (
)
